{{History of Balisca}}

The '''2021 Baliscan civil unrest''', popularly known as '''Baliscazo''' (from "Balisca" and the ''-azo'' suffix of violent augmentation) or the '''Gen Z Revolution''' (''Revolución generación Z'') refers to a series of protests, clashes, and riots between several unorganized groups throughout the [[Baliscan Confederation]]. Violence sporadically occurred predominantly between anti-statist protesters, including anarchists, [[W:Social ecology|social ecologists]] and [[Accelerationism|accelerationists]]; and pro-reformist, pro-statist, and a small number of right-wing activists. In [[Castejón]], protests progressively became more violent and multifaceted, ultimately leading the [[Metropolitan Council of Castejón|metropolitan council]] to impose a [[W:state of emergency|state of emergency]] in an attempt to combat the unrest. The unrest is considered to be the largest in Baliscan history, involving some 10 million people.

== Background ==
=== The Baliscan duo and Dagit ===
{{main|Baliscan duo}}
The execution of Baliscan citizen [[Baliscan duo|Silvestre Santmartí]] and the detention of his companion [[Quim Brugués]] by the Republic of [[Dagit]] in 2020 for drug trafficking was one of the principal triggers of the 2021 unrest. Santmartí was executed by lethal injection on 18 December 2020 under the [[Oplan Tokhang]] anti-narcotics campaign of President [[Rodrigo Tañada]], despite months of diplomatic efforts by the Palacios administration to secure clemency. Brugués was released on 15 January 2021 after the Dagiti Court of Appeals vacated his conviction on the basis of fabricated evidence—and was subsequently acquitted of all charges by a Baliscan confederal court on 23 February 2021, proving that the Dagiti evidence had been falsified and that Santmartí had been executed on the basis of planted drugs.

The case was directly overseen by DNP Chief [[Ronaldo Batot]], Tañada's longtime ally and the architect of the Oplan Tokhang campaign. Batot had personally authorized the arrest of the Baliscan duo and publicly defended the evidence chain of custody that the Baliscan court later found to be fabricated. His role in the case made him a central figure in the subsequent [[Adonian Court of Justice|ACJ]] investigation, which authorized a formal inquiry into the anti-narcotics campaign on 16 September 2021—citing the Santmartí case as evidence of a "systematic pattern of extrajudicial executions." On 13 February 2026, the ACJ included Batot among eight indirect co-perpetrators in the crimes against humanity case against Tañada, citing his role as the "chief enforcer" of the campaign.

The Palacios administration's inability to prevent Santmartí's execution—despite months of diplomatic efforts—reinforced a broader narrative that the confederal government was ineffective and out of touch with the concerns of ordinary citizens. The subsequent acquittal of Brugués in Balisca, which proved the evidence had been fabricated, intensified the outrage: the phrase ''"dues justícies"'' (Volisanian: "two justices") became one of the defining slogans of the 2021 protests.

Deputy Secretary General [[Zavier Bilal]] led the Baliscan response to Dagit's execution of the Baliscan duo, which ultimately resulted in confederal sanctions against Dagiti government officials, including Batot and Senior Deputy Prosecutor Marcial Albay. His response to the crisis was widely praised by the Baliscan public, leading to significant rises in his approval ratings. Bilal and several other ministers later broke with Palacios and her government's official position, aligning himself with the Indignados movement. This led to a schism within the government that forced Palacios to resign from both the premiership and her seat in Congress on July 27, passing the premiership to Bilal.

After an online petition posted in June had attracted nearly 4 million signatures, mass demonstrations began on 11 July. The movement was initially motivated by the [[Baliscan duo|death of Silvestre Santmartí]], increasing cost of living, and economic inequality; it asserted that the Baliscan government was no longer working for the well being of the public. The protesters successfully pushed for {{W|Economic liberalization|the rolling back of the market liberalization reforms}}, deeper confederal and [[W:Direct democracy|direct democratic]] reform, {{W|climate justice}} and [[Immigration to Balisca|immigration reform]], among other things. On 13 July, a list of 50 demands was made public and went viral on social media, becoming de facto a structuring basis for the movement, covering a wide range of eclectic topics, mostly related to democracy, and social and fiscal justice. After Secretary General [[Andrea Palacios]] made a televised address on 27 July announcing her resignation from political office, the movement evolved, with a transition towards political {{W|decentralization}} away from Castejón, and {{W|participatory democracy|participatory}} and {{W|grassroots democracy}} became its unique revendication. On the same day, anarchists utilizing fireworks and [[W:Improvised weapon|improvised weapons]] in Castejón's [[Capitoline Hill]] forced the local [[Mozos de Escuadra]] to retreat, and declared the first autonomous commune.

Deputy Secretary General [[Zavier Bilal]] assumed the duties as SecGen immediately after [[Andrea Palacios]]' resignation. Sizeable portions of the Baliscan public, especially from the right wing, and some members of the international community alike called for the next confederal elections to be moved ahead; which ultimately [[2021 Baliscan confederal election|occurred in October]]. As a result of the elections, the {{W|neoliberal}} [[Reformist Bloc (Balisca)|Reformist Bloc]] lost all of it's seats, while the number {{W|moderate}} and {{W|conservative}} delegates elected was significantly reduced, winning elections in only 23 [[Commune district|commune districts]] (out of 736), and the electorate rallying around {{W|anti-capitalist}}, {{W|anti-statism|anti-statist}} and [[Baliscan Accelerationism|accelerationist]] candidates. It was under Bilal's government that the referendum to approve the new Constitution was held on 23 July 2022, which pass with the support of 66% of the electorate.

